    description: 'I built it to behave like the environment client code will run in, without the consequences. The rate limiter is enforced and its headers are CORS-exposed so a browser can read them, auth is cookie-based JWT with rotating refresh tokens and replay rejection, writes persist, and dependency failure is modelled. Both third-party dependencies sit behind circuit breakers that fail in opposite directions on purpose. The service cannot work without Postgres, so it fails closed with 503 plus Retry-After; the rate limiter fails open to in-memory counters, because a limiter outage should not do more damage than the abuse it guards against. Neon\'s libpq connection string is normalised for asyncpg at the boundary, preserving libpq\'s semantics for sslmode=require (encrypt, not verify) and detecting the -pooler endpoint so asyncpg\'s prepared statements do not collide behind PgBouncer. It seeds roughly 9,000 rows on first boot under a Postgres advisory lock so parallel instances cannot double-seed.',
